#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:1
msgid "Fundamentals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:3
msgid ""
"PyBaMM (Python Battery Mathematical Modelling) is an open-source battery "
"simulation package written in Python. Our mission is to accelerate battery "
"modelling research by providing open-source tools for multi-institutional, "
"interdisciplinary collaboration. Broadly, PyBaMM consists of"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:8
msgid "a framework for writing and solving systems of differential equations,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:9
msgid "a library of battery models and parameters, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:10
msgid ""
"specialized tools for simulating battery-specific experiments and "
"visualizing the results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:12
msgid ""
"Together, these enable flexible model definitions and fast battery "
"simulations, allowing users to explore the effect of different battery "
"designs and modeling assumptions under a variety of operating scenarios."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:15
msgid ""
"**NOTE**: This user-guide is a work-in-progress, we hope that this brief but "
"incomplete overview will be useful to you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:17
msgid "Core framework"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:19
msgid ""
"The core of the framework is a custom computer algebra system to define "
"mathematical equations, and a domain specific modeling language to combine "
"these equations into systems of differential equations (usually partial "
"differential equations for variables depending on space and time). The "
"[expression tree](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/docs/"
"source/examples/notebooks/expression_tree/expression-tree.ipynb) example "
"gives an introduction to the computer algebra system, and the [Getting "
"Started](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/tree/main/docs/source/"
"examples/notebooks/getting_started/) tutorials walk through creating models "
"of increasing complexity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:25
msgid ""
"Once a model has been defined symbolically, PyBaMM solves it using the "
"Method of Lines. First, the equations are discretised in the spatial "
"dimension, using the finite volume method. Then, the resulting system is "
"solved using third-party numerical solvers. Depending on the form of the "
"model, the system can be ordinary differential equations (ODEs) (if only "
"`model.rhs` is defined), or algebraic equations (if only `model.algebraic` "
"is defined), or differential-algebraic equations (DAEs) (if both `model.rhs` "
"and `model.algebraic` are defined). Jupyter notebooks explaining the solvers "
"can be found [here](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/tree/main/docs/"
"source/examples/notebooks/solvers)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:27
msgid "Model and Parameter Library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:29
msgid ""
"PyBaMM contains an extensive library of battery models and parameters. The "
"bulk of the library consists of models for lithium-ion, but there are also "
"some other chemistries (lead-acid, lithium metal). Models are first divided "
"broadly into common named models of varying complexity, such as the single "
"particle model (SPM) or Doyle-Fuller-Newman model (DFN). Most options can be "
"applied to any model, but some are model-specific (an error will be raised "
"if you attempt to set an option is not compatible with a model). See []"
"(base_battery_model) for a list of options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:35
msgid ""
"The parameter library is simply a collection of python files each defining a "
"complete set of parameters for a particular battery chemistry, covering all "
"major lithium-ion chemistries (NMC, LFP, NCA, ...). External parameter sets "
"can be linked using entry points (see [](parameter_sets))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:39
msgid "Battery-specific tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:41
msgid ""
"One of PyBaMM's unique features is the `Experiment` class, which allows "
"users to define synthetic experiments using simple instructions in English"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:43
msgid ""
"pybamm.Experiment(\n"
"    [\n"
"        (\n"
"            \"Discharge at C/10 for 10 hours or until 3.3 V\",\n"
"            \"Rest for 1 hour\",\n"
"            \"Charge at 1 A until 4.1 V\",\n"
"            \"Hold at 4.1 V until 50 mA\",\n"
"            \"Rest for 1 hour\",\n"
"        )\n"
"    ]\n"
"    * 3,\n"
")\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:58
msgid ""
"The above instruction will conduct a standard discharge / rest / charge / "
"rest cycle three times, with a 10 hour discharge and 1 hour rest at the end "
"of each cycle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:60
msgid ""
"The `Simulation` class handles simulating an `Experiment`, as well as "
"calculating additional outputs such as capacity as a function of cycle "
"number. For example, the following code will simulate the experiment above "
"and plot the standard output variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:62
msgid ""
"import pybamm\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"# load model and parameter values\n"
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, experiment=experiment)\n"
"solution = sim.solve()\n"
"solution.plot()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:73
msgid "Finally, PyBaMM provides custom visualization tools:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:75
msgid ""
"[](quick_plot): for easily plotting simulation outputs in a grid, including "
"comparing multiple simulations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:76
msgid ""
"[](pybamm.plot_voltage_components): for plotting the component "
"overpotentials that make up a voltage curve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:78
msgid ""
"Users are not limited to these tools and can plot the output of a simulation "
"solution by accessing the underlying numpy array for the solution variables "
"as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:80
msgid "solution[\"variable name\"].data\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/index.md:84
msgid "and using the plotting library of their choice."
msgstr ""
